Z||000003||batboy||12-09-1999||11:11 PM||||An eight inch subwoofer in a small sealed box will fit behind each seat without modification. Mount them up a few inches off the floor so that there's a little more room to slide the seat back farther. This is before I installed the grill cover and mounted it into the Batmobile.<P> <IMG SRC="http://sites.netscape.net/batboybain/batsubbox.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000004||Monkeyman||12-10-1999||03:55 AM||||batdude--How much seat travel will you lose doing it your way?  I'm 6'2" and I can't afford to lose much more that an inch or so.||||reg||5
Z||000005||batboy||12-10-1999||06:59 AM||||I'm guessing you lose about 2-3 inches of travel and you can't recline quite as much. I'm fairly short (5'8"), so that doesn't bother me. If you just put one sub behind the passenger seat and the amp behind the driver seat, you wouldn't lose hardly any travel at all.||||reg||1
Z||000006||spike||12-10-1999||09:15 AM||||I had 2 10 inch kenwood subs in my Fiero, they were not behind the seat nor hidden. I did not loose any seat travel at all. This custom box will be available next summer. I will be making a 2 by 12 inch version of this box as well, with again no affect to seat travel.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Monkeyman||12-10-1999||01:39 PM||||batguy--I've heard that amps get pretty hot.  Is this true?  If so, what happens when the back of my seat rests on the amp?  I'd hate to have a fire, especially with all the hubub about Fieros being matches on wheels.  If this wouldn't make a difference, how would it sound with just one sub?  Wouldn't you need 2 for an equal sound or would one work just as well?  I'd hate to be lopsided! <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000008||Alex4mula||12-10-1999||03:35 PM||||A good 8" sub with a mono 150W amp will give you plenty of good bass in a Fiero. Unless you want to be noticeable in your neighborhood anything bigger will be a waste. I have a Rockford Fostgate in a 1cf box under the dash (floor) and a Jensen 150W amp. I would buy those boxes for behind the seat if I really do not loose any travel (6' driver)||||reg||10
Z||000009||olympic||12-10-1999||03:44 PM||||The top of an amp should not have anything pressed against it. This is where all the heat is dissapated from. It probably wont cause a fire but the amp will go into thermal protection and shut down. Also, 1 sub will sound just fine if it is crossed over below 100 Hz. Low bass is omni-directional so it's hard to tell where it eminates from.||||reg||1
Z||000010||william_thorniii||12-10-1999||08:21 PM||||Monkeyman,<P>I have a single front firing 10" Polk db in my passenger side footwell.  It is powered by a MTX Pro-75X4 amp mounted behind the pass. seat.  When going back and forth to Michigan (about a 4.5 hour drive) I never had any problems with excessive heat generation.  Furthermore, I had my car cranked at a party for about 6 hrs straight w/o any problems.  There is much more airflow back there than you think since it is where the air return is located that vents out to the 1/4 windows.  Also that ledge that comes down from the window can be easily removed, or customized to make the amp sit flush in there.  <P>William T. Thorn, III||||reg||1
Z||000011||Monkeyman||12-10-1999||09:00 PM||||Bill 3--I'd like to take a look at that next time I'm in your area.  (Might be the 23rd eve.  I'll email you when I know.)  <P>olympic--I got lost after the crossed stuff.  If it isn't too complicated, could you explain what all that means?  I have a cass. head unit, FM modulated CD changer and a 300 watt amp along with new, stock sized spkrs and a pair of good 6x9s.  None of it is in the car yet so I'm still making plans.||||reg||1
Z||000012||william_thorniii||12-10-1999||09:24 PM||||Monkeyman,<P>I know that you asked Olympic... but here goes.  "Crossed" was used to mean cross-over point.  Any sound can be represented by a frequency.  To keep things simple we will say the audible range is from 20-20,000 Hz.  The lower the frequency the longer the wavelength and therefore the lower the sound reproduced.  Any true subwoofer should only reproduce sounds from the max of 100Hz(usually 80Hz) down as far as it can.  Anything higher will require the woofer to move more rapid than is possible.  So what is done to make your system sound good you separate the frequencies to the individual speakers.  My .75" tweeters receive only high frequencies (20kHz-4kHz) while my 6.25" receive only midrange frequencies (4kHz-80Hz) and my Subwoofer only receives frequencies below 80Hz.  A speakers range over which it can reproduce sound is called its frequency response.  Supposedly I have a range of 20-22,000Hz.  Note that the last (highest) 2000Hz are not in the audible range of most humans.  They do however affect harmonics... very complex.  Anyway the way that various frequencies are distribued is via. a crossover.  Many amplifiers have an active crossover integrated.  Otherwise when purchasing a component set a passive (non-powered) one is provided.  They are simply high and low pass filters (cap & resister in series or parallel).  Well that is essentially the long of it... I hope that it helps.  <P>Thanks again Cliff for the bandwidth,<P>William T. Thorn, III||||reg||1
Z||000013||batboy||12-11-1999||12:00 AM||||Dang William, you about lost me and I knew what you were talking about. To make things easy for Monkey and others who don't have a degree in car audio systems, just get an amp that has a built in low-pass crossover. This filters out the higher frequencies from reaching the subwoofer. The newer, good, brand name amps have huge cooling fins and don't really get that warm. Certainly not hot enough to start a fire, just don't completely cover them up. They need a little air circulating past the fins. A single subwoofer doesn't sound lobsided. I'm running a single sub in three different vehicles.||||reg||1
Z||000014||Monkeyman||12-11-1999||12:26 AM||||I already have most of my components.  I hope I bought the right ones.  Probably not, though.  I have an amp.  I'll have to look tomorrow and see if it has a built in low-pass crossover.  When I bought my speakers, I never thought about adding a sub, so I got 3-ways.  I don't think I can return them (some dumb-a** return policy), so...can I still use them with a subwoofer?  How much would a decent sub cost and what should I look for?  I'm not building a competition system, I just want a good, full bodied sound.||||reg||1
Z||000015||batboy||12-11-1999||09:01 AM||||Keep the 3-way speakers you have. They don't have anything to do with your subwoofer(s). In fact the 3-ways give you a better range in sound frequencies, which you indicated that you want. A subwoofer will extend that range down into the low end (bass) much farther than any regular sized speaker ever can. Since you have a 86 Fiero (I think), does that mean you're putting 4" X 6" speakers in the back and doing some modifiying for 6" X 9" speakers to fit in the front? Is your amp a four channel that you were planning on running all four of these speakers? If so, that would be ideal. But, then if you do decide to get a sub, I'd recommend another dedicated amp (one with a low pass crossover) just for the sub. Two amps will fit behind the seat ok. Just get a 2 channel amp and bridge it for the sub (assuming you only put one sub in). Bridging an amp means running both outputs to the same speaker (subwoofer in this case) which increases your power to that one speaker. If you're looking for a small sub that will fit in a small sealed box, you can't go wrong with the 8 inch Rockford Fosgate Punch series sub. It's an efficient, great sounding sub that doesn't require massive amounts of power and will fit into a box that doesn't have much volume (like 0.3 cubic feet). I bought mine for about $60 recently. Hope this helps and is clear as mud.<p>[This message has been edited by batboy (edited 12-11-1999).]||||reg||1
Z||000016||Monkeyman||12-11-1999||07:03 PM||||batboy--Thanx. I'm with you.  I hadn't planned on replacing my dash speakers with the 6x9s, though.  I bought good replacement 4x10s and 4x6s and already had the new 6x9s.  I though about trying to find a way of using the 6x9s in addition to the others, but, now that I think about it, I'll probably just replace the 4x10s.  I just hate to waste the money I spent on them.  I can't return them, either.  Any suggestions?  Anybody want to make any interesting trades?  I'll post something about this in the Mall after I hear from you, batboy.  BTW-my amp is a 4-way whatever you said.  Any suggestions on what amp to buy for the sub?  How much?  My budget is running low.  I don't need a competition system, just one that sounds good.<p>[This message has been edited by Monkeyman (edited 12-11-1999).]||||reg||1
Z||000017||Monkeyman||12-11-1999||07:22 PM||||I'm interested in buying an '85 GT.  Do they have the rear speakers in the headrests or the sailpanels?  If they're in the headrests, what size are they and can I still hook up 4x6s in the sailpanels?||||reg||1
Z||000018||william_thorniii||12-11-1999||09:53 PM||||Monkeyman,<P>The 85's have the headrest speakers.  The size of the speakers in there are 3.5".  This is a very common size.  Let me ask you this.. are your 4X6 plates one oblong cone with a separate tweeter mounted above it's surface on the same centerline?  If so, I would say sell them and use the $ to buy some 3.5" speakers.  These can get fairly expensive though.  Your best bet for your needs is to start reading these car-stereo forums.  Those kids will buy and sell 10G's worth of equiptment/year.  You can often find some good deals there.  Otherwise ask the guys down at the stereo shop if they know anyone that might have some used.  You could easily enough mod. your 85 GT by putting the interior trim from a 86+ year in there.  They have provisions for the 4X6 plates if you want to use them.  In fact, if you decide to do that let me know.  Perhaps we could swap interior pieces.  My 87 has the 4X6's up there, and I don't even have them hooked up.  I am quite happy with my current system but I wouldn't mind getting rid of the speaker openings.  <P>As far as Amps go, you really can't cut corners there.  I strongly reccomend MTX for a mid $ amp.  Unfortunately amps are something that you can't go really cheap on... anything less expensive than that is probably not going to have enough #$$ to push your sub.  I found this out the hard way, but just couldn't pass up a deal.  Coupled with a JBL Sub, you should be able to get a reasonable deal on it all.  For mid-range stuff these brands (MTX, JBL) seem to have good value and quality.  If you get a 10" sub, plan on needing about 200 Watts (min)to push it although each sub has specific requirements.  A good place to head is Circuit City.. they have books with all of that stuff in it or go to <A HREF="http://www.Crutchfield.com." TARGET=_blank>www.Crutchfield.com.</A>   There you can look all of this stuff up.  Also, feel free to ask me, I will try to help you out in any way that I can.<P>William T. Thorn, III ||||reg||10
Z||000019||geyser||12-12-1999||01:44 AM||||Subs need more amp power than any other speaker in your system. They also need to have the right enclosure, sealed or ported, to create those chest pounding lows. Take some time to research your speaker box. You might be disapointed with the results without the proper enclosure, but you will definitely be pleased if you do it right.||||reg||1
Z||000020||Monkeyman||12-12-1999||03:27 AM||||Thanx, guys.  Don't know if I can get the '85 or not.  This guy's been real slow to respond to me, but he only lives about an hour and a half, so I might just pay him a surprise visit.  (Good body, custom paint, decent int., some options that work, etc.-for $500-but the motors blown)  <P>William--If I do get the car, I'll definately have to get together with you about the interior swap.  I can't picture 3.5" speakers putting out the same sound as 4x6s.  BTW--the new 4x10s and 4x6s came from Circuit City in Ft. Wayne.  I don't care for the employees there, but they have some good prices.||||reg||1
Z||000021||batboy||12-12-1999||09:29 AM||||The later model 4" X 6" rear speakers are a vast improvement over the small headrest speakers. I've heard you can mount the 4" X 6" speakers into the early Fieros with just a little modification. If you had the interior sail trim pieces from a later model car, it's even easier.||||reg||1
